Re-add GNU Hurd kernel support to lib/systems.

Restores the support that lived in nixpkgs from ~2012 through 2018 and was
removed in PR #45721 (commit history under pkgs/os-specific/gnu/).  This
patch is purely additive (no existing behaviour changes) and just makes
nixpkgs willing to PARSE `i686-gnu` / `x86_64-gnu` / `*-pc-gnu` /
`*-hurd-gnu` triplets and produce canonical `<cpu>-gnu` configs from them.
The actual glibc-hurd, hurd-headers, and hurd userland packages live in
this flake under flakes/{hurd-toolchain,hurd-headers,hurd}/.

Triplet choice: `i686-gnu` / `x86_64-gnu` is the canonical user-facing
form across the entire gnumach/MIG/Hurd ecosystem — upstream
gnumach building.html, upstream MIG building.html, cross-hurd's
CROSS_HURD_TARGET, Debian's DEB_HOST_GNU_TYPE for hurd-i386/hurd-amd64,
Sergey Bugaev's upstream glibc submissions, and Guix's %hurd-systems
all use the 2-tuple `<cpu>-gnu` form.  `<cpu>-pc-gnu` is what config.sub
canonicalises that to internally for autotools; `<cpu>-unknown-gnu` is a
pre-canonicalisation artefact of config.guess never used as an input.

Changes:
  - lib/systems/parse.nix: add the `hurd` kernel (ELF, no family,
    `.name = "gnu"`).  Add `gnu` as an alias (mirrors
    `darwin = kernels.macos`) so 2-tuple `i686-gnu` resolves.  Add
    `gnu`/`hurd` to the 3-tuple cpu-vendor-os allow-list so
    `i686-pc-gnu` doesn't hit the ambiguous-3-component throw.
    Special-case `tripleFromSystem` to emit the canonical 2-tuple
    `<cpu>-gnu` (no vendor, no abi suffix) for Hurd targets — this
    drives stdenv.cc.targetPrefix → cross-binaries `i686-gnu-gcc`,
    matching cross-hurd's bin naming.
  - lib/systems/inspect.nix: add `isHurd` predicate (gets it free via
    `matchAnyAttrs`).  Include `isHurd` in `isUnix`.
  - lib/systems/default.nix: `isHurd → libc = "glibc"` in the libc
    cascade; `|| isHurd` in `hasSharedLibraries`; uname.system entry
    `gnu = "GNU"` (keyed by parsed.kernel.name, which is "gnu").

The patch is small (~50 lines across 3 files) so refreshing it on a
nixpkgs bump is mechanical.  Verified against nixpkgs nixos-25.11
(rev 25f53830...) — the line numbers below are stable across the 25.11
branch but expect a one-line fuzz on next minor bumps.

--- a/lib/systems/parse.nix
+++ b/lib/systems/parse.nix
@@ -617,6 +617,19 @@
       windows = {
         execFormat = pe;
         families = { };
+      };
+      # GNU Hurd.  `.name = "gnu"` matches the upstream canonical naming
+      # used by gnumach/MIG building docs, cross-hurd's CROSS_HURD_TARGET,
+      # Debian's DEB_HOST_GNU_TYPE for hurd-i386 / hurd-amd64, Sergey
+      # Bugaev's glibc upstream submissions, and Guix's %hurd-systems.
+      # The kernel attribute is still named `hurd` (so `kernels.hurd` and
+      # the `isHurd` predicate read naturally) but `.name` — which feeds
+      # tripleFromSystem → config → cc.targetPrefix — is "gnu" so
+      # cross-binaries land as `i686-gnu-gcc` (not `i686-hurd-gcc`).
+      hurd = {
+        execFormat = elf;
+        families = { };
+        name = "gnu";
       };
       cygwin = {
         execFormat = pe;
@@ -642,6 +655,10 @@
       watchos = kernels.ios;
       tvos = kernels.ios;
       win32 = kernels.windows;
+      # `gnu` resolves to the Hurd kernel — used by 2-tuple parsing of
+      # `i686-gnu`, the form upstream gnumach/MIG/Hurd documentation
+      # uses on its configure invocations.
+      gnu = kernels.hurd;
     };
 
   ################################################################################
@@ -815,8 +832,22 @@
             kernel = elemAt l 1;
           };
       "3" =
-        # cpu-kernel-environment
+        # GNU Hurd: `<cpu>-<vendor>-gnu` (e.g. i686-pc-gnu, i686-unknown-gnu).
+        # Must be checked BEFORE the cpu-kernel-environment branch below —
+        # that branch's `elem (elemAt l 2) [... "gnu" ...]` check would
+        # otherwise mis-match Hurd's `gnu` as the abi field with
+        # kernel=<vendor>, which then fails with "Unknown kernel: pc".
         if
+          elemAt l 2 == "gnu"
+          && (elemAt l 1 == "pc" || elemAt l 1 == "unknown")
+        then
+          {
+            cpu = elemAt l 0;
+            vendor = elemAt l 1;
+            kernel = "gnu";
+          }
+        # cpu-kernel-environment
+        else if
           elemAt l 1 == "linux"
           || elem (elemAt l 2) [
             "eabi"
@@ -839,6 +870,8 @@
             "mmixware"
             "ghcjs"
             "mingw32"
+            "gnu"
+            "hurd"
           ]
           || hasPrefix "freebsd" (elemAt l 2)
           || hasPrefix "netbsd" (elemAt l 2)
@@ -962,7 +995,18 @@
       optAbi = optionalString (abi != abis.unknown) "-${abi.name}";
       cpuName = if kernel.families ? darwin then darwinArch cpu else cpu.name;
     in
-    "${cpuName}-${vendor.name}-${kernelName kernel}${optExecFormat}${optAbi}";
+    # GNU Hurd uses the 2-tuple form `i686-gnu` / `x86_64-gnu` everywhere
+    # in the upstream ecosystem (gnumach docs, MIG docs, cross-hurd,
+    # Debian's DEB_HOST_GNU_TYPE for hurd-i386/hurd-amd64, Sergey
+    # Bugaev's upstream submissions, Guix manifests).  config.sub
+    # canonicalises `i686-gnu` → `i686-pc-gnu` internally for autotools,
+    # but the user-facing prefix (driving cc.targetPrefix) stays as the
+    # 2-tuple — so cross-binaries are named `i686-gnu-gcc`, matching
+    # what every other Hurd consumer expects.
+    if kernel == kernels.hurd then
+      "${cpuName}-${kernel.name}"
+    else
+      "${cpuName}-${vendor.name}-${kernelName kernel}${optExecFormat}${optAbi}";
 
   ################################################################################
 
--- a/lib/systems/inspect.nix
+++ b/lib/systems/inspect.nix
@@ -300,6 +300,9 @@
       kernel = {
         families = { inherit (kernelFamilies) darwin; };
       };
+    };
+    isHurd = {
+      kernel = kernels.hurd;
     };
     isUnix = [
       isBSD
@@ -308,6 +311,7 @@
       isSunOS
       isCygwin
       isRedox
+      isHurd
     ];
 
     isMacOS = {
--- a/lib/systems/default.nix
+++ b/lib/systems/default.nix
@@ -154,6 +154,8 @@
             null
           else if final.isNone then
             "newlib"
+          else if final.isHurd then
+            "glibc"
           # TODO(@Ericson2314) think more about other operating systems
           else
             "native/impure";
@@ -214,6 +216,9 @@
               wasi = "Wasi";
               redox = "Redox";
               genode = "Genode";
+              # GNU Hurd: parsed.kernel.name is "gnu" (see kernels.hurd
+              # in parse.nix); uname -s on real Hurd reports "GNU".
+              gnu = "GNU";
             }
             .${final.parsed.kernel.name} or null;
 
@@ -245,6 +250,7 @@
           (
             isAndroid
             || isGnu
+            || isHurd
             || isMusl # Linux (allows multiple libcs)
             || isDarwin
             || isSunOS
